WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.940
GIL STRANG: Typically, the
first few days of class,

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:08.920
these guys ask what's the
class average going to be?

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.620
How are we going to be graded?

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.110
I don't have any
answers for that stuff.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.440
So I say what is totally
true, that I don't feel

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.850
my main job is to grade them.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:22.470
My job is to teach them
or learn with them.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.410
That's what I continue to
do, and gradually, they

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.925
begin to believe.

00:00:29.502 --> 00:00:30.960
SARAH HANSEN: Today
in the podcast,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.710
we're talking with teaching
legend Professor Gil Strang.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.310
GIL STRANG: Maybe the key
point is that make it human.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.970
You're a person like
the student is a person.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:46.338
The book isn't quite a person,
but it was written by a person.

00:00:46.338 --> 00:00:47.880
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome
to Chalk Radio,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.580
a podcast about inspired
teaching at MIT.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.690
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.150
One of OCW's most
popular courses

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.170
is Professor Strang's
18.06 Linear Algebra,

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.560
a key foundation for his new
course on machine learning,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.920
in which he's teaching
students to teach computers.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.830
Professor Strang is known
for inspiring students

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.060
through his teaching.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.760
One YouTube commenter
sums it up well.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.280
Quote: "This is not lecture.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.600
This is art."

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.580
We wanted to talk
with Professor Strang

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.670
to see how he's been able to
make complex math concepts

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.380
engaging and accessible.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.890
We'll pick up our conversation
with his explanation

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.850
of what his new course,
18.065 Matrix Methods in Data

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.380
Analysis, Signal Processing, and
Machine Learning is all about.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.120
GIL STRANG: So this
is my adventure

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.980
into the subject
of deep learning.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.500
For example,
recognizing an image,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.430
recognizing a zip code,
a bunch of numbers,

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:57.500
translating languages,
or playing chess,

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.780
so that's what the
course is about.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.520
How does the machine learn?

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.050
Essentially, the idea
is say take an image,

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.350
then the deep learning
system leads the machine

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.560
to look at the examples.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.040
We all learn from examples.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.980
The machine learns
from examples,

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:23.330
and from many examples,
or many chess games

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:28.490
or many pages of Chinese,
you learn what's happening.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:33.860
And what the math part is is
that the machine ultimately

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.150
tries to assign a certain
weight to a certain number,

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:42.390
big or small, to each
part of the image.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.650
So perhaps, if you're
drawing a three,

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:51.220
the computer recognizes a
three, of course, by the curves

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.340
and gives less weight,
or zero weight sometimes,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.230
to the empty space around the
three but picks out that three.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.032
So that's the idea
of deep learning.

00:03:04.032 --> 00:03:05.740
SARAH HANSEN:
Traditionally, math courses

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:09.800
have been defined by testing,
which honestly makes sense.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.550
There's typically a right
and a wrong answer in math.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.010
If you know the operation,
if you do it right,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.600
you should get the answer.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.390
Tests can be a great vehicle
for strengthening and measuring

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.860
students' skills, but Professor
Strang's approach is different.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:25.950
GIL STRANG: So I ask
everybody to do a project.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.550
There is no final exam.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.320
Actually, there
is no exam at all.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.410
I shouldn't like
say this, but that's

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.800
really what the subject is
is having an idea of how--

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.430
OK, I'll use deep
learning for some thing.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:44.140
Like the recent proposed
project was can you identify

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.370
what makes an image or
a picture attractive?

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:49.120
SARAH HANSEN: Hmm.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.580
GIL STRANG: So
somebody has to say,

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.630
these pictures are attractive.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:54.280
These are not.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.940
We have to tell the
computer something.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.800
SARAH HANSEN: What did
that feel like to try

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.560
something new, pedagogically?

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.630
GIL STRANG: Oh, it's fun.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:08.170
I like teaching, and this
is a subject where students

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.060
just come from everywhere.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.430
Because they know
what stuff to learn,

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:13.900
and they've heard about it.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.519
And some of them
know more than me,

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:20.700
and then those students
write even better projects.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.180
Yeah.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.630
So I do the lectures for
the first three quarters

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:29.800
of the course, and then I try
to get them to present which

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.600
is a great experience for them.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.240
So it takes a little
urging to get them,

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.430
but yeah, it's really
just wonderful.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:40.930
SARAH HANSEN: What
insights have you

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.070
gained about having more
of a student-led course

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:48.110
and a project-based course?

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.160
GIL STRANG: You realize,
slowly but finally,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.850
that that's how people
learn, by doing.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:58.480
You couldn't give them
a better way to learn

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.210
than create a project.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:04.180
Usually it's on some
topic they know about

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:07.600
or they they're interested in.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.620
Like how do you find a
criminal in a bunch of people?

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.350
Yeah.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.830
It's a very effective
way to learn,

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:18.100
and it's something
that gets remembered.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.750
Where doing exam questions
that I might make up,

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.150
sort of mathy
questions, I don't know

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.190
if that's remembered
10 years later,

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.240
but I think people's
projects are.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.990
SARAH HANSEN: Along
with this new approach

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.050
comes a new paradigm for
measuring student learning.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.050
Projects involve more than
right and wrong answers.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:41.410
Projects are subjective, and
bringing the subjectivity

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.530
into a math course comes
with some initial skepticism,

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.710
especially from students who
are so used to the typical

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:51.377
"learn the subject, perform on
the test" way of doing things.

00:05:51.377 --> 00:05:52.960
One of the things
that makes Professor

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.210
Strang and his
courses so special

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:59.500
is that he's not attached
to these paradigms.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.060
In 18.065, in one
of the videos, you

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.712
talk about grading
students' work.

00:06:06.712 --> 00:06:07.420
GIL STRANG: Yeah.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.510
SARAH HANSEN: And you tell
them that, although this

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:12.740
is important, to
grade their work,

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.800
it's not your main concern.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.390
That your main concern is
actually learning with them.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:18.140
GIL STRANG: Right.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:19.460
SARAH HANSEN: Could you talk
a little bit about that?

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:20.168
GIL STRANG: Yeah.

00:06:20.168 --> 00:06:21.640
That's right.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:26.100
So typically, the first few
days of class, these guys

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.780
ask, what's the class
average going to be?

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:31.480
How are we going to be graded?

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.000
I don't have any
answers for that stuff.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.410
So I say what is totally
true, that I don't feel

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.210
my main job is to grade them.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:44.680
My job is to teach them
or learn with them,

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:48.010
and that's what
I continue to do,

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:51.910
and gradually they
begin to believe.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.090
At the beginning,
they still think,

00:06:55.090 --> 00:07:00.250
OK, but he's got to give
me a B or C or an A,

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:08.210
but really that's not what
18.065 is about, a grade.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.400
It's just not.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:11.530
Math is something you do.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:12.640
You don't just read.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:13.810
You have to do it.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.550
You have to think about it.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.530
The way to learn math
is to get into it

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:23.650
and work on a thing
which takes some thought.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:29.980
You don't see it immediately,
but you see it eventually.

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:31.730
SARAH HANSEN: One of
my favorite takeaways

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:35.570
from Professor Strang's approach
is that he centers his lessons

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.000
around the humans in his class.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:41.180
For him, it's about engaging
with the students in his course

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:46.250
as people, and the learning
is done by everyone.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.430
GIL STRANG: Well, first, I like
students, and I want to help.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:55.400
And maybe the key point
is to think with them,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.620
not to just say, OK, here it is.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:58.340
Listen.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:07:59.240
Listen up.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:05.950
I think through the question
all over again, as they do,

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:07.190
and you have to give time.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:10.790
You can't zip through a
proof, because the class

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:13.160
has to be thinking with you.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:17.900
And it happens that
I lose the thread,

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.740
or I come up to a dead
end, where I don't know

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.070
what I'm supposed to do next.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.695
But well, that's
OK, because students

00:08:24.695 --> 00:08:25.820
are going to hit dead ends.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:31.580
So it seems to me it's OK for me
to get stuck too and then given

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:34.610
they see, oh, OK,
maybe this is the way

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:36.530
to get out of that corner.

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:42.039
I suppose, I try to think
it through once again,

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:46.890
and then you automatically
see the word.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.590
You recognize what
words you need to use

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:51.860
and what the steps are.

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.480
Yeah.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:55.440
If you're not
thinking it yourself,

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:57.860
then you're probably
going too fast

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:04.240
and not connecting with
the thinking of the class.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.210
And of course, you don't
know what everybody

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:10.600
is thinking in that
class, but overall,

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:14.420
if you stay conscious
of the class,

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:16.310
conscious of where they are.

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:20.120
That's I think the
same for any speaker

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:22.300
is to be conscious
of the audience,

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:27.830
and it's maybe the key
point is to make it human.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:30.790
You're a person, like
the student is a person.

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:32.810
The book isn't quite
a person, but it

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:37.820
was written by a person,
and to see that it's just

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.942
like a natural thing to do.

00:09:39.942 --> 00:09:41.150
Yeah.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:45.210
So essentially, I think the
thing is, I like students,

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:49.400
I like math, and putting
them together is just

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:51.121
the best job in the world.

00:09:51.121 --> 00:09:54.558
